WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:07.837
Look at an animal. What has
it got? Scales, feathers, fur?

2
00:00:08.057 --> 00:00:12.143
Those are features, and features
put every animal into a group.

3
00:00:12.643 --> 00:00:16.498
Scientists sort animals into
six groups, by their features.

4
00:00:16.718 --> 00:00:22.012
A feature is something the animal
has: scales, feathers, fur, six legs.

5
00:00:22.232 --> 00:00:26.133
Not where it lives. Not how it
moves. Look at the animal itself.

6
00:00:26.633 --> 00:00:30.348
Fish have scales and fins, and
they breathe through gills.

7
00:00:30.568 --> 00:00:34.562
A fish lives in water all
its life. A salmon. A shark.

8
00:00:34.782 --> 00:00:38.729
Amphibians have smooth, damp
skin, with no scales at all.

9
00:00:38.949 --> 00:00:43.129
They start life in water, as
tadpoles. Frogs, toads and newts.

10
00:00:43.349 --> 00:00:46.460
When they are grown, they
come onto land as well.

11
00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:51.604
Reptiles have dry, scaly skin.
Snakes, lizards and tortoises.

12
00:00:51.824 --> 00:00:55.586
Most reptiles lay tough
eggs on land, not in water.

13
00:00:55.806 --> 00:00:59.010
Birds have feathers and a
beak, and they lay eggs.

14
00:00:59.230 --> 00:01:02.946
Every bird has feathers, even
a penguin that cannot fly.

15
00:01:03.446 --> 00:01:06.882
Mammals have fur or hair, and
they feed their babies milk.

16
00:01:07.102 --> 00:01:10.678
Dogs, whales and bats
are mammals. So are you.

17
00:01:10.898 --> 00:01:15.078
Insects have six legs.
Count them: always six.

18
00:01:15.298 --> 00:01:20.824
An insect's body is in three
parts: head, thorax and abdomen.

19
00:01:21.044 --> 00:01:26.106
Ants, bees and beetles are insects. A
spider has eight legs, so it is not.

20
00:01:26.606 --> 00:01:31.250
Now sort. Ask about the skin, the
legs, and how it feeds its young.

21
00:01:31.470 --> 00:01:35.696
A whale lives in the sea, but it
feeds its calf milk. A mammal.

22
00:01:35.916 --> 00:01:39.492
A penguin swims, and it has
feathers. That makes it a bird.

23
00:01:39.712 --> 00:01:44.031
A bat flies, and it has fur
and milk. A mammal that flies.

24
00:01:44.251 --> 00:01:47.594
Some groups are easy to mix
up. Then you look it up.

25
00:01:47.814 --> 00:01:52.783
A fact card is a secondary source:
what somebody else wrote down.

26
00:01:53.003 --> 00:01:57.462
A crocodile lives in rivers, but
it has dry scales. A reptile.

27
00:01:57.962 --> 00:02:01.630
A diagram is a drawing with
labels that name the parts.

28
00:02:01.850 --> 00:02:05.937
It is one kind of model in
science. You will label a beetle.

29
00:02:06.157 --> 00:02:08.386
The head has the eyes and the mouth.

30
00:02:08.606 --> 00:02:13.668
The thorax is the middle part, not
the whole body. Six legs join there.

31
00:02:13.888 --> 00:02:17.232
The abdomen is the back
part, where the food goes.

32
00:02:17.452 --> 00:02:21.399
Two antennae for feeling and
smelling, and wings on the thorax.

33
00:02:21.899 --> 00:02:26.032
Fish breathe with gills.
Amphibians have smooth, damp skin.

34
00:02:26.252 --> 00:02:29.968
Reptiles have dry scales.
Birds have feathers and a beak.

35
00:02:30.188 --> 00:02:33.810
Mammals feed their babies
milk. Insects have six legs.

36
00:02:34.030 --> 00:02:39.092
Sort by the features. Look up the
tricky ones, and label your diagram.
